# Ref Integrity

Git-aware referential integrity. **Single source of logic:** `.harness/lib/ref_integrity.py` (SRP: one
responsibility — no live reference points to a renamed/deleted path). Three thin adapters
consume the same script (DRY):

- **pre-commit** — `.githooks/pre-commit --staged`. The file is always materialized in this project; the
  activation (`git config core.hooksPath .githooks`) runs on its own in a post-gen Copier task when the
  destination was already a git repository at the time of `copy`/`update` (fail-open — it only warns, does not fail, if
  it was not yet one). If this project has `use_pre_commit_framework=true`, the task is turned off on
  purpose (it would collide with `pre-commit install`) — in that case the equivalent staged hook is the
  `ref-integrity-staged` in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`.
- **this skill** — `--range`, on demand.
- **the maintenance loop** — `.claude/loop.md --since` (always installed alongside this skill, since
  the two live under `.claude/`).

See `docs/SCHEMA.md` §5 ("git is the archive").

## When to use

After renaming/moving/deleting files (curation, naming migration, prune), or when reviewing a PR/range,
to ensure no md/json/txt/py/sh/toml/css still cites the old name.

## How to run

```bash
# a range of commits (e.g. since before the curation)
python3 .harness/lib/ref_integrity.py --range <BASE>..HEAD
# since a ref (loop usage)
python3 .harness/lib/ref_integrity.py --since <ref>
# what is staged right now (the same thing the pre-commit runs)
python3 .harness/lib/ref_integrity.py --staged
# negative test of the detector itself (a real dead link MUST flag; inside a code fence it must NOT)
python3 .harness/lib/ref_integrity.py --selftest
# machine output
python3 .harness/lib/ref_integrity.py --range <BASE>..HEAD --json
```

Exit 1 = there is a broken reference; 0 = clean. Two checks: `[link]` (markdown link that doesn't resolve) and
`[stale]` (live citation to a name/path of a file renamed/deleted in the diff). A link/citation INSIDE a
code fence (` ``` `) is an example — it does not flag (`blank_code_fences`); a link with a space/accent resolves via
`%20`/unquote.
{%- if use_github_ci %} CI (`.github/workflows/docs-integrity.yaml`, installed because
`use_github_ci=true`) runs `--selftest` + the push/PR range as an additional backstop.
{%- else %} This project has `use_github_ci=false` — there is no CI backstop; pre-commit + the maintenance
loop are the only lines of defense.
{%- endif %}

## What to do with each finding

- **clean rename** (finding shows `new: <path>`) → repoint the citation to the new path.
- **delete with no new** (`new: —`) → disable the link (keep the text + a note "history in git") or remove
  the pointer in a comment; NEVER repoint to a different doc (misleading link).
- **known false positive** (generator auto-output, manual backup, historical stray) → add the term
  in `.ref-integrity-allowlist` (1 per line) with a comment explaining why.

## Exempt sources (by design, not reported)

`docs/_arquivo/`, `docs/adr/`, `docs/auditorias/`, `docs/qa-evidence/` (snapshots/immutable), `**/log.md`
(append-only), `.understand-anything/` (in any subdirectory{% if harness_understand_apps_root %} — includes
`{{ harness_understand_apps_root }}/.understand-anything/`, this project's PROJECT_ROOT for Understand
Anything{% endif %}) and `.trash-*` (scratch). They are a historical record: a citation to an old name there is legitimate.

## Guardrail

The pre-commit is fail-fast but bypassable (`--no-verify`); the `--since` loop is the backstop. It is not a substitute for
review — it is the deterministic guard that stops a rename from letting an orphan ref enter the repo.
